<Project>

  <!-- ====================================================================================
       Warning policy.

       This file exists rather than Directory.Build.props because the condition below reads
       $(IsPackable), which projects set in their own body. props is imported BEFORE the
       project body and would see it empty; targets is imported after.

       WHY THERE IS A POLICY AT ALL. Multi-targeting a library with <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
       and no #if made the nullable warning surface depend on the target framework, because
       the reference assemblies differ: netstandard2.0 and net48 are unannotated, net6.0 and
       later are annotated, so the compiler can see nullability mismatches on modern targets
       that it cannot see on legacy ones. A clean rebuild of src/JsonSettings gives:

           netstandard2.0   32 warning sites
           net48            32
           net6.0           69
           net8.0           69
           net10.0          69

       Those 35-odd extra warnings are not new defects introduced by the retarget; they are
       pre-existing nullability gaps that only became visible once an annotated BCL was in
       the picture. CS8765 fires only on net6.0+ (DynamicSettingsBag overrides), CS8604 only
       on netstandard2.0/net48.

       The same mechanism is why the SYSLIB obsoletions and the Roslyn analyzers had never
       been evaluated against this code at all before the retarget. Those did turn up real
       defects (a discarded Stream.Read return value corrupting an initialization vector,
       and a PBKDF2 call whose SHA-1 PRF was an invisible default rather than a documented
       file-format constant) and they are fixed rather than allowlisted. SYSLIB0051 is the
       one obsoletion deliberately kept: InvalidVersionException's serialization constructor
       exists for .NET Framework binary-serialization compatibility.

       WHAT THE POLICY DOES. Warnings are errors, with an explicit allowlist of exactly the
       codes the repository emits today. The debt stays visible as warnings and nothing is
       silenced, but a NEW KIND of problem fails the build instead of scrolling past in a
       log that already prints hundreds of lines. The counts above are additionally frozen
       by .github/check-warning-baseline.ps1, which fails if the number of sites grows, so
       new instances of the existing codes cannot accumulate either.

       SHRINKING THE ALLOWLIST is the way to pay the debt down: fix every site for one code,
       delete that code from the list, and it can never come back.

       NOT COVERED: src/JsonSettings.Autosave does not set <Nullable>enable</Nullable> at
       all, which is why it reports 0 sites rather than a real number. Enabling it there is
       a separate piece of work with its own warning wave, deliberately not bundled here.
       ==================================================================================== -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' != 'false'">
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!-- Every code src/ emits as of the 2.1.0 baseline. One concern per line.
           CS0109      member does not hide an inherited member; 'new' is redundant
           CS0168      variable declared but never used
           CS0219      variable assigned but its value is never used
           CS86xx      nullable reference type analysis
           CS87xx      nullable annotation/constraint mismatches on overrides and interfaces
           SYSLIB0051  formatter-based serialization ctor, kept for .NET Framework compat -->
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>
      $(WarningsNotAsErrors);
      CS0109;CS0168;CS0219;
      CS8600;CS8601;CS8602;CS8603;CS8604;CS8618;CS8619;CS8620;CS8622;CS8625;
      CS8714;CS8765;CS8767;
      SYSLIB0051;
      NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904;
      NETSDK1138
    </WarningsNotAsErrors>

    <!-- The last two entries above are not debt, they are decisions:

         NU1901-NU1904 are NuGet's vulnerability advisories. They are excluded from
         warnings-as-errors because unlike every other code here, they can appear with NO
         change to this repository: a CVE published overnight against an already-pinned
         dependency would otherwise break every developer's next build in the middle of
         unrelated work. They stay loud as warnings, and the build-and-release workflow
         fails on them in CI, which is where the decision to keep shipping or to bump
         actually gets made.

         NETSDK1138 is "this target framework is out of support", which net6.0 now is. That
         is a deliberate choice - see the TargetFrameworks in both shipped projects - so the
         reminder should stay visible without breaking a build over a decision already
         taken. It is pre-allowed rather than added on the day it starts firing: the SDK
         does not currently emit it for a net6.0 LIBRARY, only for the net6.0-windows
         Examples app, and a future SDK deciding otherwise should not be a surprise
         build break. -->

  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- ====================================================================================
       Apply the packaged build targets (out-of-process weave + re-signing) to this
       repository's own projects too.

       src/JsonSettings.Autosave and src/JsonSettings.NotifyChanges each declare aspects, and
       tests/JsonSettings.Tests and the examples consume them, so those projects are rewritten by
       AspectInjector after CoreCompile — which means they need BOTH parts of the packaged targets:
       the out-of-process weave (AspectInjector 2.9.0's in-process task leaks file handles into the
       MSBuild node; see docs/aspectinjector-2.9.0-apphost-lock.md — inside this repository the
       symptom was reused worker nodes keeping obj/bin dlls locked and failing unrelated rebuilds)
       and the re-sign (weaving invalidates the strong-name signature the compiler applied).
       Consumers get these files from each nupkg's build/ and buildTransitive/ folders; our own
       build has no nupkg to get them from, so they are imported directly. One copy each, both
       paths.

       Importing both unconditionally into every project is safe:
         - the weave targets are conditioned on $(_InjectAspectsDependsOn), which only
           AspectInjector.targets sets, so projects without the weaver (src/JsonSettings itself)
           skip them;
         - the re-sign targets are conditioned on signing being active and hooked to
           AfterTargets="AspectInjector_InjectAspects", and MSBuild ignores AfterTargets naming a
           target that a given project never defines;
         - the two files use disjoint target/property names (NucsAutosave_* vs NucsNotifyChanges_*),
           and the weave-mode handshake between them (see the files) makes the FIRST import claim
           the weave and the second stay dormant, so a woven project weaves once. Both re-sign
           targets stay wired through the same handshake; where both would act, sn -R is idempotent.

       NOTE the import ORDER relative to package targets differs from a NuGet consumer:
       Directory.Build.targets is evaluated near the top of Microsoft.Common.targets, BEFORE the
       nuget.g.targets that import AspectInjector.targets. The property logic in both files is
       written to be correct in either order; keep it that way when editing them.
       ==================================================================================== -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\JsonSettings.Autosave\build\Nucs.JsonSettings.Autosave.targets" Condition="'$(NucsQaPackageConsumption)' != 'true'" />
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\JsonSettings.NotifyChanges\build\Nucs.JsonSettings.NotifyChanges.targets" Condition="'$(NucsQaPackageConsumption)' != 'true'" />

</Project>
